      "message": "FYI: I wondered about this, so I tried to bring back just the `sk_sp` changes. The problem is that the pointer is inside the `GrGLBackendTextureInfo`, which is inside the `union`. Those fields don\u0027t get initialized/constructed (because it\u0027s not defined which of the union members should be constructed). Therefore, the sk_sp has random bits in it when a newly created (but not-yet-valid) backend texture is made. In that situation, calling assign will try to copy, which will then interpret the existing (random) bits as a valid sk_sp. The sk_sp assignment will attempt to free the \"existing\" object (not really an object!).\n\nThrough that lens, it\u0027s clear why the pointer is a manual/kludgey ref-counted pointer -- we basically treat it as a valid ref, but ONLY when the existing object was also valid.",
